  "narrative": "During the 1980s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were a vibrant expression of identity and community. The Jheri curl, a popular permanent wave hairstyle, was touted as a \"wash and wear\" style that gave the wearer a glossy, loosely curled look. While it may have been marketed as easier to care for than relaxers, its impact on natural textures is not explicitly stated in the source material. Cornrows, a traditional African braiding practice, were also likely popular during this era, as they remain an important part of many African cultures and the African diaspora today. Unfortunately, documentation from the 1980s specifically regarding hairstyles and hair culture among Black and African women is limited in this source material, but it is clear that protective styles like cornrows and possibly box braids were valued for their ability to minimize manipulation and exposure to environmental elements. The natural hair movement, while emerging in the 1960s, may have also continued to influence hairstyles during this decade, although its specific impact on Black and African women's hair culture during the 1980s is not explicitly stated.",
